<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anglo-Iraqi Treaty (1930)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1930">1930</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Anglo-Iraqi Treaty of 1930 was an alliance signed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, then under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">League of Nations mandate</a>. It provided for Iraqi independence once Iraq joined the League, which happened in 1932, but kept close British influence, including air bases at Habbaniyah and Shaibah and the right to move troops across the country. Many Iraqi nationalists opposed it.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the Mesopotamian campaign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> the British Army, with Commonwealth troops, defeated the Ottoman forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, and British troops stayed on after the war. In 1920, after the partition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, the United Kingdom formally took control of the territory under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">League of Nations mandate</a>, and the Kingdom of Iraq began with the coronation of King Faisal I on 23 August 1921. The 1930 treaty built on the Anglo-Iraqi Treaty of 1922, but it reflected Iraq's greater importance to British interests after oil was found there in 1927.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty was concluded between King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V of the United Kingdom</a> and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faisal-i-of-iraq">Faisal I of Iraq</a>. High Commissioner Francis Humphrys signed for the United Kingdom and Prime Minister Nuri as-Said for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. It opened the way to Iraq's nominal independence at the end of the mandate, and its main purpose was to secure for the British a range of commercial and military rights once that independence came.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> later wrote that the treaty let the British keep air bases near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> and Habbaniya in times of peace and gave them a right of transit for military forces and supplies at all times. In time of war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> was to provide all possible facilities, including the use of its railways, rivers, ports and airways, for the passage of armed forces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> invoked the treaty to justify its invasion and occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> after the nationalist coup of 1941, whose leaders had contacts with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis powers</a> during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, and the occupation that followed lasted until the end of 1947. As the British prepared to leave, they sought a new military treaty giving them wider powers than the 1930 agreement. The Anglo-Iraqi Treaty of 1948 was approved but never came into force, because of large and violent demonstrations against it in the Al-Wathbah uprising.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anglo-Iraqi Treaty of 1930” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Anglo-Iraqi Treaty</b> of 1930, Iraq joined the League of Nations as an independent state in October 1932, ending the mandate; the Kingdom of Iraq continued with strong British ties.</blockquote><a href="/state/mandatory-iraq">Mandatory Iraq</a></li></ul></section>
